@endnode

@node ABOUT "What it does"

@{b}What it does@{ub}

Nearly all new Amiga software is published through GitHub releases, which
is awkward since there is no way to download the releases on the machine
itself: no browser for the platform can handle the CSS and JavaScript
Github.com relies on. Getting to the newest .lha usually means walking
over to a modern computer and transfer it back to your Amiga (what is
your method of choice?)

GitFetch does that part for you. Type a GitHub address or owner/repo,
pick a release and a file, and it lands on your hard drive.

It downloads, and nothing else. Unpacking and installing stay in your own
hands, with the tools you already trust.

@endnode

@node REQUIRE "Requirements"

@{b}Requirements@{ub}

    AmigaOS 3.5 or newer     for the ReAction classes
    Recommended: 68020 or better
    A screen of 640x400 or larger
    A TCP/IP stack           Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami or Genesis
    AmiSSL 5 or newer        from Aminet
    A correctly set clock    for the SSL negotiation to work

@{b}About the clock@{ub}

That last one is easy to overlook. An Amiga without a working
battery-backed clock starts in 1978, and every TLS certificate then looks
"not yet valid", so the connection fails with an error that says nothing
about dates. GitFetch checks the date before connecting and says so
plainly. It does not set the clock itself; that belongs to a proper time
program such as the NTP client built into Roadshow.

    date                       to check
    date 24-aug-26 12:00:00    to correct

Without AmiSSL, or without a reliable clock, you can still use GitFetch
through a proxy: that side needs neither.
@{" More about that " link BACKENDS}

@endnode

@node INSTALL "Installation"

@{b}Installation@{ub}

Double-click Install, or copy the GitFetch drawer anywhere you like; it
does not care where it lives.

If you copy it by hand, set the executable bit yourself:

    Protect GitFetch +e
    Protect gftest +e

The archive format does not carry that bit, and without it the Shell
refuses to start the program.

Settings are kept in ENVARC:GitFetch/ and survive a reboot.

The saved repositories are a plain text file in ENVARC:GitFetch/Bookmarks,
one per line. You can edit it by hand or copy it to another Amiga.

@endnode

@node USING "Using it"

@{b}Using it@{ub}

@{b}Fetching@{ub}

Type into the Repository field, or paste whatever you have. All of these
work:

    owner/repo
    github.com/owner/repo
    https://github.com/owner/repo
    https://github.com/owner/repo/releases/latest

Anything after the repository name is ignored, so a link straight out of a
forum post is fine. Press Fetch, or hit Return in the field.

Capitals do not matter. GitHub treats repository names as the same name
whichever way you type them, so boingball/mintprint finds
boingball/MintPRINT. Handy when you are copying a name off a screen and
are not sure how it was written.

GitFetch keeps that in mind for the saved list as well: a repository you
have already saved is not added twice just because you typed it
differently the second time.

@{b}Downloading@{ub}

Pick a release on the left; its files appear on the right. Pick a file,
choose where it should go with "Save in", and press Download. The bar
shows how far along it is, and Stop breaks it off cleanly.

If a file of that name already exists you are asked before anything is
overwritten. The same happens when the volume looks too full for the file
to fit: you can still go ahead, but at least you were warned.

The window has an iconify gadget in its title bar. Clicking it puts
GitFetch away as an icon on Workbench; a running download simply carries
on, because it happens in a process of its own.

@{b}Saved repositories@{ub}

The list starts with GitFetch itself, at MathieuSEO/GitFetch. Double-click
it to see whether there is a newer version than the one you are running.

Save puts whatever is in the Repository field into the list on the left.
Click a saved entry to put it back in the field; double-click to fetch it
straight away. Remove takes the selected one out again.

@{b}How many releases@{ub}

The chooser at the bottom decides how much is fetched: only the newest, or
up to twenty-five. Fewer is faster, and over a direct connection it saves
a great deal of traffic. @{" Why that matters " link BACKENDS}

@endnode

@node BACKENDS "Two ways to reach GitHub"

@{b}Two ways to reach GitHub with GitFetch@{ub}

There are two options to download from your favorite repo, switchable in
the settings.

@{b}Directly, through AmiSSL@{ub}

No server of your own needed. GitHub sends its full reply (around 240 KB
for 25 releases), mostly release notes that get thrown away -- and all of
it passes through TLS decryption on a 68k.

@{b}Through a proxy@{ub}

A small Python script, included, using only the standard library. It turns
the same reply into about 5 KB of plain text, already converted to the
Amiga character set, and caches it so GitHub's limit of 60 requests per
hour is not an issue. Faster and lighter, but it only works while that
server runs.

@{" Setting one up " link PROXY}

The setting for how many releases to fetch matters most on a direct
connection: "latest" costs a fraction of "all".

Fields that do not apply to your choice are greyed out: with a direct
connection the proxy details are irrelevant, and the other way round the
certificate check is.

@endnode

@node SETTINGS "Settings"

@{b}Settings@{ub}

Open with the Settings menu, or right-Amiga+I.

    Connect to GitHub directly   choose between the two ways above
    Verify certificate           check you are really talking to GitHub
    Proxy address, Port          only used when going through a proxy
    Number of releases           how many to fetch at once

Save writes them to ENVARC: so they survive a reboot. "Just now" applies
them to this session only, and Cancel leaves everything as it was.

Fields that do not apply to your choice are greyed out.

Settings can also come from the icon's tooltypes (PROXYHOST, PROXYPORT,
DESTDIR, MAXRELEASES, BACKEND) or from environment variables under
GitFetch/ in ENV:. Environment variables win, so you can try something
from the Shell without touching the icon.

@endnode

@node PROXY "The proxy"

@{b}The proxy@{ub}

The proxy is a small Python script; it needs nothing beyond the standard
library. It runs on any machine that can reach both GitHub and your Amiga:
a Linux server, a NAS, or the computer next to you.

    python3 gitfetch_proxy.py

By default it listens on 127.0.0.1 port 8080. To let your Amiga reach it:

    GITFETCH_BIND=0.0.0.0 GITFETCH_PORT=8099 python3 gitfetch_proxy.py

Then set that address and port in the GitFetch settings.

One thing to watch when putting it behind a web server: it must serve
plain HTTP. An Amiga without AmiSSL cannot follow a redirect to HTTPS, so
any automatic redirect has to be switched off for that hostname.

The full description is in README-proxy.md in the server drawer.

@endnode

@node TROUBLE "Troubleshooting"

@{b}Troubleshooting@{ub}

@{b}"The Amiga clock is wrong"@{ub}

An Amiga without a working battery-backed clock starts in 1978. Every
certificate then looks "not yet valid" and the connection fails. Check
with @{b}date@{ub} and correct it:

    date 21-aug-26 17:00:00

@{b}"Cannot connect to the server"@{ub}

Going through a proxy: is the script running, and is the address right?
Try it from another machine first.

Directly: can your Amiga resolve names? A proxy on a numeric address needs
no name server, so this may be the first time it matters.

@{b}"GitHub refused the request"@{ub}

Without a token GitHub allows sixty requests per hour. Wait, or use a
proxy, which remembers replies for a few minutes.

@{b}"AmiSSL 5 is required"@{ub}

Check with @{b}version amisslmaster.library@{ub}. Version 5 or newer is
needed; older ones lack the HTTPS client GitFetch uses.

@{b}Only one release shown@{ub}

That is the default, to keep traffic down. Change it with the chooser at
the bottom of the window.

@endnode

@node HISTORY "Version history"

@{b}Version history@{ub}

@{b}0.1@{ub}  -  first release, for my own use and for testers

    The first version that does the whole job: type a repository, pick a
    release and a file, and it lands on your hard drive.

    - ReAction interface with saved repositories
    - Two ways to reach GitHub: directly through AmiSSL, or through a
      small proxy of your own
    - Choose how many releases to fetch, to keep traffic down
    - Asks before overwriting an existing file
    - Downloads run in their own process, so the window stays responsive
      and Stop works during a transfer
    - Checks the system clock before connecting, because a wrong date
      makes TLS fail in a way that says nothing about dates
    - English interface, prepared for translation through catalogs
    - Warns when a file would not fit on the target volume
    - Can be iconified while a download runs

@endnode

@node AUTHOR "Author"

@{b}Author@{ub}

GitFetch was written by Mathieu Burgerhout.

    @{"https://github.com/MathieuSEO/GitFetch" system "URLOpen https://github.com/MathieuSEO/GitFetch"}

Which is also where new versions appear, and where GitFetch can fetch them
itself: the repository is in the saved list from the start.

Built with bebbo's amiga-gcc and the AmigaOS 3.9 NDK.

